  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge will continue to shift eastward and become \ncentered over the Gulf Coast states late this week as a trough \ndevelops over the western CONUS. Tempertures remain slightly above \nnormal as we remain on the western edge of the ridge. Highs in the \nmid 90s to lower triple digits are expected Thursday and Friday. \nThough triple digit temperatures become confined to portions of the \nTrans Pecos and along the Big Bend on Friday. Afternoon/evening \nshower and thunderstorm chances (20-60%) increase late this week \nacross the western higher terrain as shortwave impulses move \nthrough southwest flow aloft. These chances expand from southeast \nNew Mexico to the Rio Grande, with the best chances (50-70%) over \nthe Davis Mountains and Marfa Plateau. Dry conditions are maintained \nacross the eastern half of our CWA due to its closer proximity to \nthe ridge. Locally heavy rainfall, frequent lighting, and gusty \nwinds will be the main hazards with the strongest of storms. Long-\nrange model guidance and cluster analysis shows the upper-level \nridge building back to the west this weekend. As such, the latest \nforecast shows a decrease in storm chances and become mainly \nconfined to the higher terrain.\n  \nGreening",
